WIBTA for telling my husband I don't want any of us to go to his cousin's graduation party? We're in our 30s with 2 kids. Our oldest is named Sam.  Sam is from my 1st marriage, which ended with me being single and homeless living with family. I got no help from my ex (still don't). I was on assistance for a small time, but living with sketchy family on welfare wasn't a good life for Sam. I made the choice to  drop out of school and work to making a better living short term. I had enough units to teach preschool, so I found a job that paid enough for us to get off public assistance, got a 1 bedroom and babysat overnight to give Sam a living with no gunshots. I met my (now) husband 4 years later.

&#x200B;

10 years later he's now my husband and we also have a 4 year old, Dan. My husband is the best man you'd ever meet. He's loved Sam as his own since they met. He's taken care of us, supported us, encouraged Sam's bio dad to better his life-not to mention that he keeps a better house then I ever could alone. Basically he's the perfect guy.

&#x200B;

2 years ago we decided together that I should finish school. I'd have more earning power and it's always been my dream to teach. So that's what we did. I chose a school where I can take classes remotely (I still worked too) and he picked up more hours at work. He's worked in retail his whole life-sometimes working 14 hour shifts all so I could finish school while us having minimal student loan  debt. Well I'm done and graduation is in June. My husband wants to pursue his dream-school part time while being a stay-at-home dad. I was offered a job  that makes more then both of us combined, but it's a bit far so we will have to relocate.  Financially we can do it, but we can't afford to do that and attend my graduation. (It's in an expensive city across the state)

Of course I insisted that we pick moving. I shrugged off walking in my graduation because my husband sees that as MORE IMPORTANT then him being able to pursue his dream right away. We're officially moving and I'm so happy for this.

But my husband's youngest cousin is also graduating with her credential from (a different) school. She's going to be a teacher as well and her family is having a big party to celebrate. They live 2 hours from here. His whole family will be there and he has not seen them in a while. I do not want to go.

&#x200B;

I'm jealous. We worked so hard to get what we have and walking was a part of my dream. I was devastated when I stopped school and I never thought I'd have a chance to make it right. I don't want to celebrate someone else's success knowing I couldn't celebrate my own.

I'm insisting that we don't go but I'm getting the feeling from my husband that his family thinks I'm the asshole. He's supportive, but I'm not sure if I'm being petty.

&#x200B;

Should I push aside my ego and just celebrate her accomplishment?

&#x200B;

EDIT:

I don't think I worded it correctly in my original post. My graduation is on a Saturday in June. Hers is the week before and her party is on the day mine was supposed to be. Only her parents are going to see her walk.

&#x200B;

Not sure if that makes a difference, but I think I'm going to try to go to the party. Husband refuses to go without me. 100% will. not. go.

&#x200B;

Edit2:

His words were "I won't be going without you and you know that". We are a 'we'. So my choice is essentially our choice.

